    <h2 id="section-2" style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.5rem; margin-top: 30px;">2. Housing Society Deemed Conveyance: Statutory Importance & Procedure</h2>
    <p>In multi-family apartment projects and housing societies across India (most notably governed under the <strong>Maharashtra Ownership Flats Act - MOFA</strong> and state cooperative society laws), a developer is statutorily mandated to transfer the legal title of the land plot and common areas to the registered Cooperative Housing Society (CHS) within 4 months of society incorporation.</p>
    <p>When a developer neglects or refuses to execute a voluntary Conveyance Deed, the housing society can file a legal application for <strong>Deemed Conveyance</strong> before the District Deputy Registrar (DDR) / Competent Authority. Upon conducting hearings and inspecting flat buyer agreements, the Competent Authority issues an official Deemed Conveyance Order, enabling the society to unilaterally register the land conveyance deed at the SRO and update government revenue records (Property Card / City Survey Records / PR Card).</p>

    <h2 id="section-3" style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.5rem; margin-top: 30px;">3. Essential Covenants & Drafting Standards for a Legal Conveyance Deed</h2>
    <p>To withstand judicial scrutiny, a conveyance deed must contain well-drafted legal clauses covering title, boundaries, and indemnities:</p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Title Recitals & Chain of Custody:</strong> Unbroken chronological trace of title deeds for a minimum 30-year period detailing how the transferor acquired ownership (Sale, Gift, Inheritance, Partition).</li>
        <li><strong>Unencumbered Warranty Clause:</strong> Express covenant by the transferor declaring that the property is free from all mortgages, bank liens, attachment orders, tax liabilities, and litigation.</li>
        <li><strong>Schedule of Property & Boundary Map:</strong> Detailed physical identification including survey numbers, door numbers, municipal ward classifications, plot area, and cardinal boundaries (North, South, East, West).</li>
        <li><strong>Indemnity Covenant for Title Losses:</strong> Legal undertaking by the grantor promising to compensate and indemnify the grantee against any future financial or legal loss resulting from prior title defects.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="section-4" style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.5rem; margin-top: 30px;">4. SRO Registration Procedure and Post-Registration EC Verification</h2>
    <p>Registering a Conveyance Deed involves presenting the executed deed on appropriate state e-stamp paper at the jurisdictional Sub-Registrar Office, completing biometric fingerprint scanning and photo capture, and paying prescribed stamp duty (5% - 8%) and registration fees (1%).</p>
    <p>Following SRO registration, property owners must search the official state Encumbrance Certificate portal (TNREGINET, Kaveri 2.0, IGRS AP, Dharani TS) after 7 to 14 days to confirm that the new conveyance entry appears in SRO Register Index II records.</p>

    <h2 id="section-5" style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.5rem; margin-top: 30px;">5. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
